  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Monday)\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA more active pattern is forecast for the long term period as \ntroughing over the western US helps direct tropical moisture from \nVance across the eastern Pacific northward over the southern Plains \nby late Thursday into Friday. Even though it appears most of the \narea can expect at least some rain (PoPs >60% Friday into Friday \nnight) ensemble guidance continues to favor southwest/western \nportions of the area for the highest rainfall totals. Probabilities \nfor at least an inch of rain are currently around 50-70% across \nsouthwest OK and western north TX but taper off to only about 10-20% \nalong and east of I-35. The wave is expected to exit to the \nnortheast early on Saturday, with drier conditions now expected \nby Saturday afternoon and continue into Sunday. \n\nModels then show an amplification of the western US trough as it \nmoves into the central US later Sunday into early next week. Details \nremain unclear at this range but at the very least expect additional \nrain chances to return to the area. Depending how the system evolves \nwill also have to watch for the potential for severe weather, heavy \nrain, and/or high winds if the higher amplitude model solutions come \nto fruition. \n\nWare",
